{% import 'chrome/macros.html.j2' as c %}
<section>
  <h2>Telling the User</h2>

  <p>Capability that varies by browser has to be communicated, and the ways to get this wrong
  are well documented. The governing rule already exists in Bucketer's own design notes: the
  interface must never claim progress it cannot observe.</p>

  <h3>Five principles, from how other products handle it</h3>

  <table class="kv">
    <tr>
      <td>Gate the feature, not the application</td>
      <td>Document editors gate only offline mode; a web code editor gates local folder access
          while the editor still loads. Blocking an entire application is defensible only when
          no fallback exists. Here one always does.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Describe the layer, not the deficiency</td>
      <td>Public-sector service guidance frames progressive enhancement as what each layer
          provides, never as a judgment on the user's choice of browser.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Disclose before commitment</td>
      <td>The job panel already lists the folder and its size before offering to start.
          Capability belongs in that same moment, not at the point of failure.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Never invent a percentage</td>
      <td>Version control tools show a real bounded count in place of an unknowable one.
          Browsers themselves fall back to bytes-received when a total is unavailable. Honest
          partial information beats invented precision.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Explain disabled controls inline</td>
      <td>Unexplained disabled controls are a documented top complaint, and hover text alone
          fails for accessibility.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Where it appears</h3>

  <p>Three placements, reusing patterns the application already has: a capability line in the
  job-creation panel beside the delivery choice; the existing queue badge extended to name the
  tier in use; and an inline explanation of what this browser can and cannot do, available
  where a user would ask rather than hidden behind a hover.</p>

  <h3>Proposed wording</h3>

  <p>Factual, describing what happens rather than instructing the reader how to feel.</p>

  <blockquote>Chrome and Edge on desktop can download straight into a folder you choose, with
  progress and resume. This browser downloads files individually into your Downloads folder
  instead.</blockquote>

  <blockquote>Files arrive as a flat list — no browser can create folders when downloading from
  a webpage.</blockquote>

  <blockquote>12 of 412 files handed to your browser. Bucketer cannot see their progress or
  confirm they finished — check your browser's download list.</blockquote>

  <blockquote>Downloads over a few hundred megabytes are unreliable on phones: the browser
  stops this tab when you switch apps. For anything large, use a desktop browser or the
  command-line option.</blockquote>

  <blockquote>This job is 840 GB. A transfer tool will finish sooner and survives restarts —
  this tab cannot.</blockquote>

  <h3>What to avoid</h3>

  <p>Blocking the whole application when a fallback exists. Bare technical error strings with
  no consequence or next step. Repeating a capability notice every session rather than once.
  Detecting the browser's name instead of its capabilities. And any percentage, estimate or
  transfer rate on the tier where the application cannot see the transfer.</p>
</section>
